from lib import *

slide("The ruling", "The ruling, leaf by leaf",
'''<table style="font-size:18.5px"><tr><th style="width:62px">Leaf</th><th style="width:420px">Concealed, or imposed without informed consent</th><th style="width:380px">Disclosed and freely accepted</th><th>What the maker must do</th><th style="width:130px">Mark</th></tr>
<tr><td class="k">A1</td><td><b>Forbidden</b> (deceit). The buyer may rescind and recover the price, or be compensated for the shortfall.</td><td>Permitted. A short-lived product honestly described is a lawful sale.</td><td>State the designed or rated life where the buyer sees it.</td><td>'''+chip("strong")+'''</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">A2</td><td><b>Forbidden</b> (an act on another&#8217;s property, and deceit). The owner may demand restoration and compensation.</td><td>Permitted if the consent names the effect and the owner can refuse and keep the device as it was.</td><td>Say what the update does. Offer the choice.</td><td>'''+chip("strong")+'''</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">B</td><td><b>Forbidden</b> to disable a working device or break a stated promise of support (for example, a &#8220;lifetime&#8221; service).</td><td>Ending support is permitted: hardship brings ease (art. 17).</td><td>Announce the end date at sale, or in good time. Leave the device working. Do not block others who can help.</td><td>'''+chip("strong")+'''</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">C</td><td><b>Forbidden</b> to close the owner&#8217;s means of repair in secret.</td><td><b>Disputed among the schools</b> (void in the strict ones, valid in the broad ones). We adopt the narrow rule: only a stated, specific safety need.</td><td>Do not forbid others from repairing what the owner owns. He need not supply every part.</td><td>'''+chip("probable")+'''</td></tr></table>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:12px"><b>Remedies for all leaves:</b> rescission or compensation for the buyer (the schools differ on which: TDV, &#8220;Hile&#8221;); restoration where the maker changed the device; <i class="t">taʿzīr</i> by the ruler, on which the scholars agree. The buyer must not have known of the limit, must not have accepted it after finding out, the limit must have affected the price, and the maker must have acted knowingly.</p>''',
"Remedies and conditions: TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi, &#8220;Hile&#8221; and &#8220;Ayıp&#8221;. Mecelle 17: Berki/Balcı. The narrow rule for C is our adoption of the stricter school view, marked probable for that reason.")

slide("The ruling", "What the maker must do, may do, and may not do",
'''<div class="cols" style="gap:24px"><div class="col"><div class="card teal"><div class="label">Must</div><ul class="b small" style="margin-bottom:0">
<li>Tell the buyer the designed or rated life, and the support period, where he can see it.</li>
<li>Name what an update does before it touches the owner&#8217;s device, when it reduces a function.</li>
<li>Offer a real choice when a protective measure costs performance.</li>
<li>Announce the end of support in good time.</li></ul></div></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card"><div class="label" style="color:#1B7F4B">May</div><ul class="b small" style="margin-bottom:0">
<li>Sell short-lived goods, if they are honestly described.</li>
<li>Sell budget models and premium models side by side.</li>
<li>End support with notice; leave devices working.</li>
<li>Lease, if it is called a lease and priced as one.</li>
<li>Install improving updates under a general advance consent the owner gave and can withdraw.</li></ul></div></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card amber"><div class="label" style="color:#A63030">May not</div><ul class="b small" style="margin-bottom:0">
<li>Hide a limit he knows of.</li>
<li>Slow, disable or lock a sold device without informed consent.</li>
<li>Switch off a service on which a working device depends and which was promised for its lifetime.</li>
<li>Reserve the means of repair so that the owner cannot fix what he owns, beyond a narrow stated safety need.</li></ul></div></div></div>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:16px">Nothing here requires a product to last long, to be repairable by everyone, or to be supported forever. It requires only that the buyer know and the owner remain in charge.</p>''')

slide("Testing the map", "Four public cases, placed on the map",
'''<table style="font-size:20px"><tr><th style="width:230px">Case</th><th style="width:130px">Leaf</th><th>What the tree shows</th></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Phoebus bulb cartel, 1925&#8211;1939</td><td class="k">A1</td><td>A collective choice to hold bulb life at a set number of hours. Whether buyers were told, and whether the engineering trade-off was real, decides the verdict.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Apple iPhone throttling, 2016&#8211;2020</td><td class="k">A2</td><td>A real engineering reason (necessity, partly accepted). The wrong lies in the missing disclosure and choice. The 2018 fix is the remedy this ruling prescribes.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Revolv hub shutdown, 2016</td><td class="k">B</td><td>A working device made useless by switching off the service it needs, after a &#8220;lifetime&#8221; promise.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">John Deere repair tools, 2025&#8211;2026</td><td class="k">C</td><td>Repair means reserved to the maker&#8217;s dealers. An agreed order, not a finding, ends the case.</td></tr></table>
<div class="bar amber" style="margin-top:20px"><b>What these cases are for.</b> They test whether the map can place real events, and show how the tree answers each. They do not prove the ruling; the ruling stands on the principles. Facts are taken from the cited records, and where the record is disputed, the slide says so.</div>''')

slide("Case · A1", "Phoebus, 1925: a bulb life agreed between makers",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col" style="flex:1.1"><div class="label">The record</div><ul class="b small">
<li>Phœbus S.A. was incorporated in Geneva on 15 January 1925. Members included Osram, Philips, Tungsram, Associated Electrical Industries and General Electric (through its overseas companies).</li>
<li>It set the life of a household bulb at <b>1,000 hours</b>. Sources give the earlier norm as 1,500&#8211;2,000 or 2,500 hours.</li>
<li>Members sent samples for testing and paid fines when their bulbs lasted longer than the standard.</li>
<li>Krajewski&#8217;s reconstruction reports the average life of a reference bulb falling from about 1,800 hours (1926) to about 1,205 hours (1933&#8211;34).</li>
<li>The cartel ended around 1939. The UK Monopolies Commission (1951) rejected the charge of planned obsolescence, saying there is &#8220;no absolutely right life&#8221;; engineers note that a hotter filament gives more light and burns out sooner.</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Through the tree</div><ol class="prem" style="font-size:20px"><li data-n="Q1">A choice of the makers. Yes.</li><li data-n="Q2">Acts on the product itself, before sale. <b>A1.</b></li><li data-n="D1">Harm? Yes, if buyers expected longer life.</li><li data-n="D2">Disclosed? <b>Not established in the sources.</b></li><li data-n="D3">Forced? The light-and-life trade-off is a real necessity <b>if disclosed</b>.</li></ol>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:21px"><b>Verdict.</b> The ruling does not depend on motive. It asks whether buyers were told and whether the trade-off was real. A cartel adds a second wrong, since it removes the alternative that might have given buyers a better product.</div></div></div>''',
"Sources: Wikipedia, &#8220;Phoebus cartel&#8221; (members, 1,000-hour standard, fines, 1951 commission); M. Krajewski, &#8220;The Great Lightbulb Conspiracy,&#8221; IEEE Spectrum, 2014 (figures as quoted in secondary summaries; the article itself was not opened).")

slide("Case · A2", "Apple, 2016&#8211;2020: a protective slowdown",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col" style="flex:1.1"><div class="label">The record</div><ul class="b small">
<li>Late 2016: some iPhone 6 and 6S units shut down unexpectedly. Early 2017: iOS 10.2.1 added power management to cut shutdowns.</li>
<li>18 December 2017: a benchmark developer published evidence of slower performance on updated phones.</li>
<li>About 20 December 2017: Apple confirmed that it limits peak performance according to battery health, <i class="t">&#8220;to prevent the device from unexpectedly shutting down.&#8221;</i> On 28 December it apologized and cut the battery replacement price from $79 to $29.</li>
<li>2018 (iOS 11.3): battery health display added, and the limit can be switched off.</li>
<li>France fined Apple &euro;25 million (Feb 2020) for omitting information. In the US, Apple settled a $500 million class case (Feb 2020) and paid $113 million to 34 states and DC (Nov 2020), without admitting wrongdoing. Apple has said it never intentionally shortens product life.</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Through the tree</div><ol class="prem" style="font-size:20px"><li data-n="Q1">A design decision. Yes.</li><li data-n="Q2">Acts on the device, after sale. <b>A2.</b></li><li data-n="D1">Harm? Yes, slower phones.</li><li data-n="D3">Forced? <b>Partly accepted</b>: batteries age, shutdowns are real.</li><li data-n="D2">Disclosed with a choice? <b>Not at the time</b>; later yes.</li></ol>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:21px"><b>Verdict.</b> The limit may stand; the silence may not. The 2018 switch and battery display are exactly the remedy the ruling asks for: <i>tell the owner, let him choose.</i></div></div></div>''',
"Sources: Wikipedia, &#8220;Batterygate&#8221; (timeline, prices, iOS 11.3, settlements, Apple&#8217;s statements); TechCrunch and AppleInsider reports of the French and Italian regulator fines (read as search summaries). Regulators&#8217; findings are cited as record, not as proof.")

slide("Case · B", "Revolv, 2016: a working device switched off",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col" style="flex:1.1"><div class="label">The record</div><ul class="b small">
<li>Revolv sold a smart-home hub that depended on the maker&#8217;s servers. Nest (Alphabet) acquired Revolv in October 2014 and stopped selling and developing it.</li>
<li>In April 2016 Nest announced that the hub and its app would stop working on <b>15 May 2016</b>. The hardware would cease to function.</li>
<li>The hub had been sold with a &#8220;lifetime subscription&#8221;; Nest defined the lifetime as the lifetime of the <i>device</i>, which it then ended. The one-year warranty had expired.</li>
<li>One trade-press report says affected owners were offered a refund or another remedy. A single source says so and it has not been confirmed.</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Through the tree</div><ol class="prem" style="font-size:20px"><li data-n="Q1">A choice of the maker. Yes.</li><li data-n="Q2">Acts on the product? No: it acts on the service the product needs.</li><li data-n="Q3">Does the owner need the maker&#8217;s help? Yes. <b>B.</b></li><li data-n="D1">Harm? Yes, a working device is lost.</li><li data-n="D3">Forced? A shut-down of an unmaintained service is a cost, not a necessity.</li></ol>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:21px"><b>Verdict.</b> Ending a service is lawful with notice. Switching off a device for which a &#8220;lifetime&#8221; was sold, with a few weeks&#8217; warning, breaks the promise that set the price. Remedy: refund or compensation.</div></div></div>''',
"Sources: Wikipedia, &#8220;Google Nest&#8221;; VentureBeat and The Next Web reports of April 2016; CEPro commentary on a possible refund (read as search summaries). The notice period is the interval between the April announcement and 15 May.")

slide("Case · C", "John Deere, 2025&#8211;2026: repair tools kept to dealers",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col" style="flex:1.1"><div class="label">The record</div><ul class="b small">
<li>January 2025: the Federal Trade Commission and five states (Arizona, Illinois, Michigan, Minnesota, Wisconsin) sued Deere.</li>
<li>The complaint alleged that Deere limited farmers&#8217; and independent shops&#8217; ability to repair its equipment: the only software tools able to perform all electronic repairs were offered to authorized dealers alone.</li>
<li>8 July 2026: a stipulated order was filed. For 10 years Deere must give farmers and independent repairers, on fair and reasonable terms, resources equal to those it gives dealers (fault codes, reprogramming, parts pairing, manuals) and must instruct its dealers not to discriminate or retaliate against customers who use them.</li>
<li>The order takes effect when a court approves it. The press release does not say whether that has happened, or whether Deere admitted liability; a law-firm memo reports that it did not.</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Through the tree</div><ol class="prem" style="font-size:20px"><li data-n="Q1">A choice of the maker. Yes.</li><li data-n="Q2">Acts on the product? No.</li><li data-n="Q3">Does the owner need the maker&#8217;s own help? No: the means could come from others.</li><li data-n="">The maker closes them. <b>C.</b></li><li data-n="D2">Disclosed and accepted? The complaint alleges dependence on dealers; whether buyers were told at sale is not stated in the release.</li></ol>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:21px"><b>Verdict.</b> An agreed order is not a finding. But the order&#8217;s content, equal access without retaliation, is what the ruling requires: the maker may not close the owner&#8217;s own power to repair.</div></div></div>''',
"Sources: FTC press release, 8 July 2026 (page opened); Paul Weiss client memo on the settlement (read as a search summary).")
